WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.685
DbContext lifetime should match the application pattern.

2
00:00:03.685 --> 00:00:05.001
In many ASP.

3
00:00:05.001 --> 00:00:14.739
NET Core applications, a scoped DbContext per request is a normal starting point because it keeps change tracking and unit-of-work behavior bounded.

4
00:00:14.739 --> 00:00:27.242
Background jobs, batch operations, and long-running processes may need different patterns, but those patterns should be intentional rather than accidental reuse of a request-oriented design.

5
00:00:27.242 --> 00:00:32.374
Configuration should differ across development, test, staging, and production.

6
00:00:32.374 --> 00:00:37.638
Development may need local databases, detailed diagnostics, and disposable data.

7
00:00:37.638 --> 00:00:48.693
Production needs protected credentials, stable provider settings, controlled logging, safe retry behavior, and permission boundaries that reflect real operational risk.

8
00:00:48.693 --> 00:00:52.904
Environment-specific settings should be explicit and reviewable.

9
00:00:52.904 --> 00:01:00.800
Connection strings, database credentials, certificates, managed identities, and service principals are sensitive assets.

10
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:15.408
They should be managed through approved secret-management and deployment mechanisms, not hardcoded in source, committed to ordinary configuration files, printed in logs, captured in screenshots, or exposed in build output.

11
00:01:15.408 --> 00:01:20.014
A leaked connection string can become direct access to sensitive data.

12
00:01:20.014 --> 00:01:25.278
Database provider configuration and EF Core service registration deserve review.

13
00:01:25.278 --> 00:01:39.162
Retry settings, command timeouts, detailed errors, sensitive data logging, lazy loading behavior, connection pooling, migrations configuration, and provider-specific options all influence security or operations.

14
00:01:39.162 --> 00:01:45.150
Defaults are useful, but teams should know which settings are active in production and why.

15
00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:49.296
Debug-oriented database settings should stay out of production.

16
00:01:49.296 --> 00:02:04.825
Detailed exception output, sensitive value logging, broad local permissions, automatic destructive behavior, and sample connection strings may be helpful during development, but they are inappropriate for real users and production data.

17
00:02:04.825 --> 00:02:10.747
Release review should include EF Core configuration, not only controller and service code.

18
00:02:10.747 --> 00:02:14.761
Different activities may need different database permissions.

19
00:02:14.761 --> 00:02:22.262
Runtime access, migrations, reporting, maintenance, and administration do not necessarily require the same rights.

20
00:02:22.262 --> 00:02:35.159
Separating those responsibilities where appropriate makes it easier to reduce blast radius and understand who or what can change data structures, read broad data, or perform privileged operations.
